(;FF[4]CA[UTF-8]GM[1]SZ[19]GN[Jiang Qirun's Qidan creation. Difficulty factor: 9.5. This question is a global question, using the Chinese rules of black stickers 3 and 3/4. The number of Bamboo joint squares is the same, and there are no raises. It is solved by Jie Zheng Practical Play. No matter what method Black Go uses, the ultimate goal is to win the round. This question starts with White GoLife and Death in the middle, and will eventually lead to a transition to the Endgame situation, involving the special situation of Chinese rules Lower "sticking to the robber to collect the queen" (if Black or White can resist the robberies and fight to the last single official, the Lower can be converted into a one-shot advantage when one's own team collects the queen). Note: Adapted from Practical Play by Jiang Qirun VS Yan Zaiming. Modified from Q-145887]GC[Type: Appreciation question | Level: 6D+ | Rating: 4.0/5 | Correct: 0% (0/1) | Desc: Jiang Qirun's Qidan creation. Difficulty factor: 9.5. This question is a global question, using the Chinese rules of black stickers 3 and 3/4. The number of Bamboo joint squares is the same, and there are no raises. It is solved by Jie Zheng Practical Play. No matter what method Black Go uses, the ultimate goal is to win the round. This question starts with White GoLife and Death in the middle, and will eventually lead to a transition to the Endgame situation, involving the special situation of Chinese rules Lower "sticking to the robber to collect the queen" (if Black or White can resist the robberies and fight to the last single official, the Lower can be converted into a one-shot advantage when one's own team collects the queen). Note: Adapted from Practical Play by Jiang Qirun VS Yan Zaiming. Modified from Q-145887 | First move: Black]AB[ia]AB[ja]AB[ka]AB[la]AB[ib]AB[hc]AB[ic]AB[jc]AB[qc]AB[rc]AB[fd]AB[gd]AB[hd]AB[jd]AB[kd]AB[ld]AB[md]AB[nd]AB[pd]AB[qd]AB[rd]AB[ee]AB[ge]AB[ne]AB[pe]AB[bf]AB[ef]AB[ff]AB[gf]AB[mf]AB[of]AB[pf]AB[bg]AB